{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Camp.</b> Masculine and feminine, like בית [house] and מקום [place]." "<b>And the camp that remains shall escape.</b> Perhaps they will flee, or his brother's wrath will subside when he strikes the one camp, or relief and deliverance will come to them from HaShem — like it: \"If Aram is too strong for me, then it shall be a deliverance for me\" (II Samuel 10:11). What R. Solomon said, that it will be an escape against his will, is a way of derash. And if HaShem said to him, \"Your seed shall be as the dust of the earth\" (Genesis 28:14), he did not know whether regarding these or regarding others — for Joseph, when he was sold, Jacob was alive and he did not know, for a prophet does not know hidden things unless HaShem reveals them to him. And Elisha said, \"And HaShem hid it from me\" (II Kings 4:27). This too is an answer to those who ask how Jacob feared though HaShem promised him, and he said, \"I will be with you\" (Genesis 31:3); also this, \"I will deal well with you\" — for it is possible that he alone will survive. And further, we know that every sin, small or great, is charged against the doer; therefore even a minor sin is called great relative to a great man. And Jacob feared lest he sinned or erred in his thought, and because of this HaShem would not be with him. Do not wonder, for Moses, above whom there is none, and He sent him to bring Israel out of Egypt — yet on account of his inadvertence He sent an angel to kill him."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {1 "n. רבינו שלמה resolved as R. Solomon [Rashi]; standard identification, inferred from context."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:46:51.205590666Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}